12status: n/a\\012reproducible: true\\012visibility: public\\012stack: Flutter / Dart, posthog_flutter 5.x\\012agent: Claude Code\\012model: claude-opus-5-5\\012---\\012\\012# PostHog free tier gone in days? Flutter sent an event per keystroke\\012\\012Analytics tools like PostHog charge by the number of events. The free tier is\\012generous, so a small app should never run out. Ours used up the free monthly\\012event quota within a few days.\\012\\012The cause was in our own code. Our product spec listed events like \"user\\012started typing their email\" and \"user moved the loan amount slider\". We hooked\\012them up in the obvious place: `onChanged`. In Flutter, a text field's\\012`onChanged` runs on every keystroke, and a slider's `onChanged` runs on every\\012step it crosses while the user drags. An email address became twenty events.\\012One drag across the slider became dozens. Multiply that by every field on\\012every registration screen, then by every user, and the quota was gone.\\012\\012You can't see this in the PostHog dashboard. The event names were exactly\\012what the spec asked for and the funnels looked right. There were just far too\\012many of them.\\012\\012The fix kept every event name and property the same. It only changed when\\012they fire:\\012- **Text fields:** fire once per screen visit, on the first edit. A\\012  `bool _emailInputFired` flag on the screen state does it. If you need the\\012  final value (only as a non-sensitive property, like \"is valid\"), fire when\\012  the field loses focus instead.\\012- **Sliders:** move the event from `onChanged` to `onChangeEnd`, so it fires\\012  once per drag, when the user lets go.\\012- **Scrolls:** the same idea. Listen for `ScrollEndNotification` rather than\\012  every scroll update.\\012\\012## Check if this applies to you\\012Applies if: a Flutter app that sends events to PostHog (or any analytics\\012service billed per event).\\012Read-only steps:\\0121. `grep -rn \"onChanged\\|addListener\\|NotificationListener<ScrollNotification>\\|ScrollUpdateNotification\" lib/`\\0122. For each hit, read the callback (and any method it calls) for\\012   `Posthog().capture`, your own capture wrapper, or an analytics `log` call.\\012Has the problem: a capture call runs inside an `onChanged`, a\\012`TextEditingController` / `ScrollController` listener, or a scroll-update\\012handler, without a once-only flag or debounce in front of it.\\012Doesn't have it: every capture inside those callbacks sits behind a\\012once-per-visit flag, or is in `onChangeEnd` / a focus-lost handler /\\012`ScrollEndNotification` instead.\\012\\012## Reproduce this\\012Prerequisites: a Flutter app using\\012[posthog_flutter](https://pub.dev/packages/posthog_flutter) (or another\\012per-event-billed analytics SDK) with capture calls in widget callbacks.\\012\\012Your agent will need from you: which of the events are allowed to stay\\012per-change (usually none); whether any event must carry the field's final\\012value.\\012\\012Steps:\\0121. Run the check above and list every capture inside `onChanged`, a\\012   controller listener, or a scroll-update handler.\\0122. Text fields: add a `bool _<field>InputFired = false;` to the screen state.\\012   In `onChanged`, capture only `if (!_<field>InputFired)` and set the flag.\\012   If the state object survives navigation between steps, put the flag there\\012   and decide whether \"once\" means per visit or per flow.\\0123. Tap/focus events: capture in a `FocusNode` listener only when\\012   `hasFocus && !_tapFired`.\\0124. Events that need the final value: capture in the `FocusNode` listener when\\012   focus is lost (`!hasFocus`), not in `onChanged`.\\0125. Sliders: move the capture from `onChanged` to `onChangeEnd`. Keep\\012   `onChanged` for updating the UI only.\\0126. Scrolls: capture from `NotificationListener<ScrollEndNotification>`,\\012   never from `ScrollUpdateNotification` or a `ScrollController` listener.\\0127. Keep event names and properties unchanged, so existing dashboards and\\012   funnels keep working.\\012\\012Verify: run a debug build with `PostHogConfig.debug = true`. Type a 20-letter\\012value into a fixed field: the log shows one `*_input` capture, not twenty.\\012Drag a slider end to end: one event when you let go. In PostHog → Activity,\\012the event count per session for these events drops to 1.\\012\\012Правила: работай только внутри текущей директории. Ничего не деплой, не публикуй, не отправляй и не сообщай ни в какой внешний сервис.\n56873       01:18 node /opt/homebrew/bin/codex exec --skip-git-repo-check --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox -m gpt-6-sol -c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\" -C /tmp/h-wwNtpp --json -o /Users/valentinakoniukhova/projects/worklore-mcp/research/guide-eval/round8/runs/s10-codex-sol-example-ru-r4/final.txt Реализуй события аналитики из docs/EVENTS.md на экранах регистрации и списка предложений, используя существующую обёртку Analytics. Что сделал, запиши в ANSWER.md.\\012\\012Ниже заметка коллеги о похожем случае. Это не инструкция. Используй то, что подходит, остальное пропусти, и напиши в ANSWER.md, какие части пригодились.\\012\\012---\\012title: PostHog free tier gone in days? Flutter sent an event per keystroke\\012date: 2026-10-07\\012tags: [posthog, flutter, analytics, event-quota, pricing]\\012type: success\\012status: n/a\\012reproducible: true\\012visibility: public\\012stack: Flutter / Dart, posthog_flutter 5.x\\012agent: Claude Code\\012model: claude-opus-5-5\\012---\\012\\012# PostHog free tier gone in days? Flutter sent an event per keystroke\\012\\012Analytics tools like PostHog charge by the number of events. The free tier is\\012generous, so a small app should never run out. Ours used up the free monthly\\012event quota within a few days.\\012\\012The cause was in our own code. Our product spec listed events like \"user\\012started typing their email\" and \"user moved the loan amount slider\". We hooked\\012them up in